{
  "index": 3,
  "response": "The North Atlantic Treaty, also known as the Washington Treaty, was signed on April 4, 1949, and established the North Atlantic Alliance, a political and military alliance of countries in Europe and North America. The treaty consists of three main articles:\n\nArticle 1: The parties agree to settle international disputes through peaceful means and to refrain from the threat or use of force inconsistent with the purposes of the United Nations.\n\nArticle 2: The parties will work towards developing friendly international relations by strengthening their free institutions, promoting stability and well-being, and eliminating conflict in their international economic policies.\n\nArticle 3: The parties agree to maintain and develop their capacity to resist armed attack through continuous and effective self-help and mutual aid, and to consider an armed attack against one or more of them as an attack against all of them, which they will respond to by taking forthwith individual and collective action, including the use of armed force, to restore and maintain security in the North Atlantic area.\n\nIn summary, the treaty establishes a mutual defense alliance among its signatories, with the aim of promoting peaceful resolution of disputes, stability and well-being in Europe and North America, and collective defense against armed attack.",
